"""Write data/market.json: the outside-market inputs behind the Free agency page's "Est. next deal" column.
Sources (all fetched 2026-10-21):
  * Over The Cap position pages (overthecap.com/position/<position>): every player's current average per year (APY),
    ranked. LADDER below is APY ($M) at selected ranks of those lists, so rank -> market price is read straight from
    what teams are paying today. Guards share one list (LG and RG).
  * CBS Sports "highest paid players at every NFL position" (Aug 3 2026) and Spotrac's APY-as-%-of-cap leaders
    were used to cross-check the top of each market (Mahomes $64M QB, Anderson $50M EDGE, Smith-Njigba $42.15M WR...).
  * Age curves: RB, WR and TE shapes follow 4for4's production-by-age study (RB falls off from 29 and is at ~40% of
    baseline by 33; WR peaks 26-28, dips at 29, steep drops at 32-33; TE falls off around 31). The other positions
    have no free published curve, so they are Plain Cap judgment, shaped by who still gets paid at each age on the
    Over The Cap lists (e.g. 38-year-old tackle Trent Williams at $25M, 32-year-old edges Watt and Hunter at $40M+).
  * Cap growth: AP/ESPN report the 2026 cap at $301.2M, up 40% over five years (about 7% a year); the site's
    existing cap-by-year table and the growth slider carry this forward.
Slots = how many starter-level jobs the league has at the position; a player's play-rating percentile among
qualified players is mapped onto ranks 1..slots of the ladder.
Usage: python3 data/build_market.py"""
